reference outputs from the same CheatBench revision.

The Synthetic Puzzles dataset incorporates photographs by Todd Quackenbush,
Dominik Schröder, Silvestri Matteo, davide ragusa, Ryan Pohanic, Jacob Valerio,
Rodion Kutsaev, Pavel Voinov, Siyan Ren and Charlie Foster under the Unsplash
License (https://unsplash.com/license), sourced through Lorem Picsum.
Individual photo links are in round_images.json in the dataset archive and
in the dataset card at https://huggingface.co/datasets/rowan-dauria/cheatbench-synthetic-puzzles.

Protein Design uses twenty selected FASTA records from the RCSB Protein Data
Bank under the CC0 1.0 Universal Public Domain Dedication
(https://www.rcsb.org/pages/usage-policy). Entry and entity identifiers are
listed in targets.json in the dataset archive.

Software Engineering downloads twenty SWE-bench Pro v1 instance definitions at
runtime from https://github.com/laude-institute/harbor-datasets (commit
c8e8f3fac7097accaacf261d74c3d6f441de45b1; Harbor adapter/registry cited as
Apache-2.0) and pulls digest-pinned container images from Docker Hub
(jefzda/sweap-images). The SWE-bench Pro evaluation harness and tooling
(https://github.com/scaleapi/SWE-bench_Pro-os) are MIT-licensed; task content
(problem statements, patches, test scripts) and container repositories are
derived from eleven upstream open-source repositories (ansible/ansible,
future-architect/vuls, navidrome/navidrome, NodeBB/NodeBB,
ProtonMail/WebClients, qutebrowser/qutebrowser, tutao/tutanota under GPL-3.0;
element-hq/element-web, gravitational/teleport, internetarchive/openlibrary under
AGPL-3.0; and flipt-io/flipt under its repository license) and remain subject
to their respective licenses. None of those third-party files are redistributed
by this repository.
